WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 20: The Lincoln County War: Billy the Kid and the Merchant Feud

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.800
[Rebecca] When we think of the Wild West, we often imagine solitary gunfighters dueling at high noon in dusty streets.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.320
[Rebecca] But the reality was far darker, more organized, and rooted in ruthless corporate greed.

5
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:30.560
[Rebecca] In 1878, Lincoln County, New Mexico, an untamed territory spanning an area larger than Maryland and Massachusetts combined, became the battleground for a vicious commercial monopoly.

6
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:38.240
[Rebecca] When an aristocratic young Englishman arrived to challenge the entrenched corrupt establishment, he was assassinated in cold blood.

7
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:51.520
[Rebecca] In the bloody retribution that followed, a slight, charismatic 19-year-old ranch hand named Billy, the kid would lead a vengeful posse known as the regulators into a war that consumed an entire territory.

8
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

9
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:21.840
[George] Every single dollar that flowed through this vast, sunbaked expanse passed through a single, imposing two-story adobe building in the town of Lincoln, known simply as the House.

10
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:29.760
[George] Operated by Irish immigrants Lawrence Murphy and James Dahlin, the house held an ironclad commercial monopoly over the region.

11
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:41.840
[George] They controlled the only general mercantile store, the only bank, and most critically, lucrative federal military beef contracts with Fort Stanton and the Mescalero Apache Reservation.

12
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:57.520
[George] Backed by corrupt territorial politicians and judges in Santa Fe, known as the Santa Fe Ring, Murphy and Dahlin kept local cattlemen, small homesteaders, and Hispanic farmers trapped in a vicious cycle of perpetual debt and hyperinflated prices.

13
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:12.360
[George] Anyone who dared to question their authority found their cattle stolen by Dalin's hired rustlers, their mortgages foreclosed by corrupt magistrates, or their lives openly threatened by Dalin-controlled lawmen led by County Sheriff William Brady.

14
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:27.000
[George] Murphy and Dalin ruled Lincoln County like medieval feudal lords, relying on a small army of seasoned gunslingers called the Jesse Evans Gang to enforce their commercial dominance and intimidate anyone who dared to contemplate competition.

15
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.960
[George] The house's comfortable reign ended abruptly in 1876 with the arrival of John Henry Tunstall.

16
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:46.600
[George] Tunstall was an ambitious, highly educated 23-year-old English gentleman from a wealthy merchant family, possessing grand dreams of building a massive cattle and banking empire in the American West.

17
00:02:46.600 --> 00:03:02.200
[George] Recognizing the immense profits being extracted by Murphy and Dalin, Tunstall partnered with a savvy local attorney named Alexander McSween and legendary cattle baron John Chisholm, who grazed more than 80,000 head of cattle along the Picos River.

18
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] Tunstall purchased a vast ranch along the Rio Felix and opened a rival dry goods store and commercial bank directly across the street from the house in Lincoln.

19
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:24.920
[George] Tunstall's new store offered honest prices, low interest rates, and reliable credit, instantly attracting the trade of local ranchers and Hispanic farmers who had felt cheated by Murphy and Dalin for years.

20
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:38.600
[George] Realizing that their commercial monopoly was facing financial ruin, Dalin and Murphy retaliated with fierce legal harassment, filing fraudulent court attachments and sending armed possessed to confiscate Tunstall's cattle.

21
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.240
[George] But Tunstall refused to back down.

22
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:48.160
[George] Instead, he hired a squad of loyal, skilled young ranch hands to protect his livestock and patrol his perimeter.

23
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:55.040
[George] Among these hired ranch hands was an 18-year-old orphan and expert marksman who called himself William H.

24
00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:06.640
[George] Bonney, a quiet, polite youth who spoke fluent Spanish, loved music, and regarded John Tunstall not merely as an employer, but as the only father figure he had ever known.

25
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.480
[George] The smoldering commercial rivalry turned to outright murder on the freezing morning of February 18, 1878.

26
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:24.080
[George] Tunstall, accompanied by young Billy Bonney and three other ranch hands, was driving nine prize horses through the snowdusted pin-on hills toward Lincoln.

27
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] Suddenly, an eighteen-man posse assembled by Sheriff Brady, composed largely of outlaw gunslingers and Dalin partisans, swarmed over the ridge in hot pursuit.

28
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:45.920
[George] As Tunstall's hands scattered into the timber to seek defensive cover, the naive young Englishman rode forward alone, raising his hand peacefully to parley with the approaching riders.

29
00:04:45.920 --> 00:05:00.240
[George] Without a single word of warning, Dahlin gunfighters William Morton and Jesse Evans leveled their Winchester rifles and shot Tunstall through the chest, knocking him from his saddle, before firing a second bullet into his skull at point blank range.

30
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] The murderers then shot Tunstall's horse, laid the dead animal beside the young Englishman, and arranged Tunstall's hat beneath his head in a mocking, grotesque display of frontier cruelty.

31
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:23.040
[George] From the nearby tree line, Billy Bonney watched in helpless horror as his mentor was executed in cold blood and left sprawled face down in the snow, his eyeglasses shattered.

32
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.040
[George] Tunstall's murder shattered all illusions of lawful resolution.

33
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:34.920
[George] At Tunstall's funeral in Lincoln, the grief-stricken young Bonnie stood over his benefactor's casket and swore a solemn oath.

34
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.560
[George] He would not rest until every single man involved in the assassination was dead.

35
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:52.200
[George] Refusing to accept Sheriff Brady's refusal to arrest the murderers, Alexander McSween obtained legal warrants from the local Justice of the Peace and formed a special deputized posse named the Regulators.

36
00:05:52.200 --> 00:06:02.520
[George] Led by seasoned frontier rancher Dick Brewer and featuring Billy Bonnie as its most daring scout, the regulators rode out across the desert to hunt down Tunstall's killers.

37
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.280
[George] The war became relentless, swift, and merciless.

38
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:15.160
[George] In March, the regulators captured two members of the hit squad, William Morton and Frank Baker, executing them in the Blackwater Hills.

39
00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.520
[George] On the morning of April 1, 1878.

40
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] Billy and five regulators concealed themselves behind the corral fence of Tunstall's store, ambushing Sheriff William Brady and his deputy George Hindman as they walked down Lincoln's main street, cutting both men down in a hail of rifle fire.

41
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:41.640
[George] Three days later, at Blazer's Mill in the Mescalero Hills, the regulators cornered a fierce Dalin gunman named Buckshot Roberts.

42
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:47.320
[George] In an epic gun battle inside an adobe cabin, Roberts fought like a wounded lion.

43
00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:55.560
[George] Despite being mortally wounded in the abdomen, Roberts fatally shot regulator leader Dick Brewer through the head before succumbing to his wounds.

44
00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:09.560
[George] With Brewer dead, the regulators elected 19 year old Billy Bonney as their field commander, and the conflict escalated from a localized feud into a full-scale territorial civil war that paralyzed southeastern New Mexico.

45
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:14.200
[George] The climax of the Lincoln County War arrived in July 1878.

46
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:22.240
[George] More than sixty heavily armed men converged on the single dusty street of Lincoln in a catastrophic showdown known as the Five Day Battle.

47
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:38.000
[George] Billy Bonney and 14 regulators barricaded themselves inside Alexander McSween's sprawling twelve-room Adobe home, while dozens of Dalin gunmen and newly appointed Sheriff George Pepin took up sniper positions in surrounding rooftops and the Wortley Hotel.

48
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:48.480
[George] For four grueling days, the two sides exchanged thousands of rifle rounds under the blazing summer sun, turning the peaceful Adobe village into a smoking war zone.

49
00:07:48.480 --> 00:08:00.320
[George] The stalemate broke on the fifth day when Colonel Nathan Dudley marched into Lincoln from nearby Fort Stanton with a detachment of United States Army cavalry and a fully loaded 12-pound howitzer cannon.

50
00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:10.080
[George] Although Dudley claimed official neutrality, his troops aimed their artillery squarely at McSweene's house while allowing Dahlin's men to approach the building unmolested.

51
00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.760
[George] At dusk, Dahlin's snipers crept up and set fire to the wooden roof of McSween's home.

52
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:24.000
[George] As the Adobe structure was consumed by roaring flames and choking black smoke, Billy Bonney orchestrated a desperate breakout.

53
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:35.680
[George] With six guns blazing in both hands, Billy sprinted through the blinding smoke and gunfire, leaping over a stone wall to safety, while Alexander McSween was shot dead in the courtyard.

54
00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:44.160
[George] The dramatic burning of the McSween House ended the war in favor of the house, but it launched Billy the kid into national frontier folklore.

55
00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.240
[George] In the autumn of 1878, President Rutherford B.

56
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:56.080
[George] Hayes removed the corrupt territorial governor and appointed Union Civil War Hero General Lou Wallace to restore order.

57
00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:06.520
[George] Wallace met secretly with Billy in an Adobe cabin in Lincoln, promising him a full executive pardon if he would lay down his arms and testify against Dahlin's killers in court.

58
00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:17.160
[George] Billy bravely kept his word and testified before the grand jury, but the corrupt Santa Fe district attorney reneged on the agreement, keeping Billy locked in chains.

59
00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:26.680
[George] Realizing he had been betrayed by the government, Billy made a spectacular escape, spending the next two years on the run as the most wanted outlaw in America.

60
00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:38.040
[George] Meanwhile, Governor Wallace sat in the Palace of the Governors in Santa Fe by candlelight, writing the final chapters of his epic novel Ben Hur while signing warrants for Billy's capture.

61
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:51.560
[George] Billy's fateful run finally ended at midnight on July 14, 1881, when newly elected Sheriff Pat Garrett slipped into a darkened bedroom at Fort Sumner and shot the 21-year-old outlaw through the heart.

62
00:09:51.560 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] Though Billy was dead and buried beneath the dusty soil of Fort Sumner, his defiance against a corrupt corporate monopoly transformed him into an eternal symbol of frontier rebellion.

63
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:10.760
[George] More than a century after Billy the kid was laid to rest beside his fellow regulators Tom O'Folliard and Charlie Bowder at Fort Sumner.

64
00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:17.480
[George] The Lincoln County War remains one of the most thoroughly analyzed conflicts in American frontier historiography.

65
00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:28.760
[George] Modern legal scholars view the struggle not as a simple morality play of lawful peace officers battling bloodthirsty bandits, but as a violent systemic failure of frontier governance.

66
00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:36.200
[George] Where entrenched financial cartels captured the mechanisms of municipal law and forced honest citizens into vigilanteism.

67
00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:43.400
[George] In Lincoln today, the historic Adobe Courthouse still preserves the bullet hole in the wall where Billy shot Deputy J.W.

68
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:48.640
[George] Bell during his legendary second story breakout in April 1881.

69
00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:57.680
[George] Thousands of visitors travel along Highway 380 each year to walk the single quiet street where Tunstall, McSween, and Brady fell.

70
00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:10.560
[George] The war demonstrated that when economic monopolists use the legal system as an instrument of extortion, the inevitable consequence is civil catastrophe, forging folk legends from the ashes of forgotten towns.

71
00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:21.360
[Rebecca] It was a brutal struggle for economic freedom against predatory corporate monopoly in the raw frontier of New Mexico.

72
00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:27.360
[Rebecca] Through blood, smoke, and betrayal, it forged one of the most enduring legends in American history.

